// TEST: Diag Tuple Param With Where Clause (diag)
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: `comm`/`anticomm` clause on a kernel that also declares a `Tuple<N>` parameter is not supported
// ============================================================================
// A `Tuple<N>` kernel parameter is realized at the apply seam by replacing it
// with its k row parameters plus a body-entry `let p = (..)` -- the same
// mechanism the defaults fill uses (docs/plan-tuples-vs-arg-packs.md 6b). That
// renumbers parameter POSITIONS and removes the tuple parameter's NAME from
// the list, and the two classes of `where` conjunct address parameters by one
// or the other. The refusal splits along exactly that line:
//
//   * `comm` / `anticomm` are POSITIONAL index lists. After the expansion a
//     surviving group lands on the wrong slot, and the failure is SILENT (a
//     misplaced comm group degrades to dense storage with no diagnostic).
//     Worse, there is nothing settled to remap TO -- "comm(p, q)" between two
//     PAIRS has no agreed meaning. Still REFUSED, and that is this file.
//
//   * The PARALLEL STRATEGIES (`omp`/`cuda`/`mpi`) resolve BY NAME to the
//     operand a parameter contributes, and under one-level structural matching
//     (6c) a `Tuple<k>` parameter is ONE schema node -- so the name still
//     designates a well-defined unit of the nest. ALLOWED since the omp-licence
//     ruling; the licence is rewritten onto the synthesized row params, and
//     `tuples/014` is the accept pin (values + emitted pragma).
//
// The escape for comm is in the message and is always available: write the
// parameters flat, one per operand.
// ============================================================================
let A: Array<Float64 like Idx<3>> = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
let B: Array<Float64 like Idx<3>> = [10.0, 20.0, 30.0]
let f = lambda(p: Tuple<2>, q) where comm(p, q) -> p[0] * p[1] * q
let g = object_for(f) <@> (A, B, A) |> compute
